de)? It creates a new proxy each time - fine.\n\nLet me now define the tracker:\n\n```ts\nclass Tracker {\n  deps: Map<string, Dependency> = new Map()\n  meta: AtomicSelectorMeta\n  addLeaf(pathParts: string[], value: any) { ... }\n  addSelector(name: string, value: any) { ... }\n  addProps(key: string, value: any) { ... }\n}\n```\n\nFor leaf deps, id = `pathParts.join('.')`. But for Map/Set/array methods, pathParts may contain non-string keys. We need special id encoding:\n- Map key access: path = ['data'] + ['map:a'] -> id `data.map:a`. So pathParts could be `['data', 'map:a']`.\n- Set: `['data', 'set:a']`.\n- Array index: `['list', '0']`.\n- Nested object: `['user', 'name']`.\n\nFor reading back from state, we need to interpret `map:a` as a Map key. So Dependency.path should store structured segments: `{ type: 'prop', key: 'user' } | { type: 'mapKey', key: 'a' } | { type: 'setValue', value: 'a' } | { type: 'arrayIndex', index: 0 }`. Let's use structured path segments.\n\nDependency:\n```ts\ninterface DepSegment {\n  kind: 'prop' | 'index' | 'mapKey' | 'setValue'\n  key?: string | number\n  value?: any\n}\n```\n\nId for health: join segment display strings: prop -> key, index -> String(index), mapKey -> `map:${key}`, setValue -> `set:${value}`. So `['data', mapKey 'a']` -> `data.map:a`. For reading, use segments.\n\nNow the proxy handler for **plain objects/arrays**:\n\n`get(target, prop, receiver)`:\n- If prop === ATOMIC_RAW_SYMBOL: return target.\n- If prop is a symbol (like `Symbol.iterator`): return Reflect.get(target, prop, target). For arrays, `Symbol.iterator` is used by spread/for-of; we need to track all elements when iterated. We can wrap the iterator so each `next()` records index. Let's handle: if prop === Symbol.iterator, return a wrapped iterator function that, when called, iterates and records each element access (with proxy values). Similarly for `Symbol.asyncIterator` maybe not needed.\n- If Array and is array index (`isArrayIndex(prop)`): record 