hether to put the engine state on context or module. Since `resetContext` should clear it, context is best. I'll add to `Context` type.\n\nLet me write the proxy handling. This is the trickiest part.\n\n### Proxy implementation details\n\nI'll define constants:\n```ts\nconst RAW = Symbol('kea.atomic.raw')\nconst LOGIC_STATE = Symbol('kea.atomic.logicState') // maybe not needed\n```\n\nHelper `isAtomicProxy(v): boolean` - we can check if `v && typeof v === 'object' && v[RAW] !== undefined`. But accessing `v[RAW]` on a foreign proxy might trigger its get trap. That's fine; our get trap returns raw. For plain objects, `v[RAW]` is undefined. Let's use `unwrapProxy`:\n```ts\nfunction unwrapProxy(v: any): any {\n  if (v && (typeof v === 'object' || typeof v === 'function') && v[RAW] !== undefined) {\n    return v[RAW]\n  }\n  return v\n}\n```\nBut `v[RAW]` on a regular object just returns undefined. On our proxy, the get trap intercepts and returns `target`. Good.\n\nWait, there's a problem: if `v` is a `Map` or `Set`, our get trap for the `RAW` symbol must return the raw target, and for other symbols like `Symbol.iterator` must return the wrapped iterator. Let me handle symbol prop at the top of get.\n\n`createStateProxy(raw, baseSegments: DepSegment[], ls): any`:\n- If raw is null or not object/function, return raw.\n- If `raw[RAW] !== undefined` (already our proxy), return raw (avoid double-wrapping).\n- If Array.isArray(raw): return new Proxy(raw, arrayHandler(ls, baseSegments))\n- If raw instanceof Map: return new Proxy(raw, mapHandler(ls, baseSegments))\n- If raw instanceof Set: return new Proxy(raw, setHandler(ls, baseSegments))\n- else: return new Proxy(raw, objectHandler(ls, baseSegments))\n\nThe handler's `get`:\n```ts\nfunction get(target, prop, receiver) {\n  if (prop === RAW) return target\n  if (typeof prop === 'symbol') {\n    // for Symbol.iterator on arrays/maps/sets, return wrapped iterator\n    ...\n    return Reflect.get(target, prop, target)\n  }\n  const value = Reflect.get(target, prop, target)\n  // record dependency\n  trackerFor(ls)?.addLeaf([...baseSegments, propSegment(prop)], value)\n  // return tracked value\n  return wrapReadValue(ls, value, [...baseSegments, propSegment(prop)])\n}\n```\n\n`propSegment(prop)`:\n- If target is array and prop is array index (string '0' etc): `{ kind: 'index', key: Number(prop) }`.\n- Else: `{ kind: 'prop', key: prop }`.\n\n`trackerFor(ls)` returns the currently active tracker if it belongs to the same logic. But proxies from a different logic (foreign) shouldn't record into the current logic's tracker with wrong base. Actually, a state proxy from logic A, when read during evaluation of logic B's selector, should record... hmm. If logic B connects logic A's selector `user` (a reducer selector), `getInputValue` unwraps the foreign proxy and wraps it in a foreign selector proxy, so the compute never sees logic A's state proxy. Good. So state proxies from A only get read during A's own evaluations. But what if user code calls `logicA.selectors.user()` outside evaluation? `trackerFor` returns null, so no recording. Good.\n\nBut what if logic A's state proxy is read during logic B's evaluation because B's compute receives it directly (e.g., B connects A's *whole* root via `from: '*'`)? Then `getInputValue` would call A's root selector which returns A's state proxy; we unwrap and wrap in foreign proxy. So B's compute sees foreign proxy. Good.\n\nSo `trackerFor(ls)` should return the active tracker if `activeTracker.logic === ls`? Since proxies are created with `ls` (the logic state they belong to), and the active tracker is also for a specific `ls`. Let's store `ls` on the tracker. Proxy handler has `ls` and checks `activeTracker && activeTracker.ls === ls`. But during nested evaluation of the same logic's selectors, activeTracker changes. When a proxy created during selector A's eval is read during A's compute (within A's tracker), ls matches. When it's read later during B's eval (same logic), ls matches too. Good.\n\nWait, but there's a case: reducer selector `user`'s proxy is created during `user`'s evaluation. Later, `userName`'s compute reads `user.name` via that proxy. The active tracker is `userName`'s, ls same => records `user.name` into userName's tracker. Good.\n\nNow, `wrapReadValue(ls, value, path)`:\n- `const raw = unwrapProxy(value)` - if value is already a proxy (e.g. a Map method returns an object that was previously proxied), unwrap.\n- If raw is object/array/map/set, return `createStateProxy(raw, path, ls)`.\n- Else return raw.\n\nBut creating a new proxy for every read can be expensive and breaks identity. Let's add a proxy cache: `const proxyCache = new WeakMap<object, WeakMap<string, any>>()`? Actually we need cache keyed by raw object + path string. Let's use a `Map` from path string to proxy per logic state? Hmm, simpler: per AtomicLogicState, a `WeakMap<object, Map<string, any>>`. But raw objects can be shared across logic states. Let's keep a global WeakMap from raw object -> Map<pathString, proxy>. Since the same raw object at the same path should give the same proxy. Different logic states with same raw object but different base path strings get different proxies. Good.\n\n```ts\nconst proxyCache = new WeakMap<object, Map<string, any>>()\nfunction cachedProxy(raw, pathString, create) {\n  let m = proxyCache.get(raw)\n  if (!m) { m = new Map(); proxyCache.set(raw, m) }\n  let p = m.get(pathString)\n  if (!p) { p = create(); m.set(pathString, p) }\n  return p\n}\n```\n\nBut the proxy handler depends on `ls` and `baseSegments`; if we cache by pathString and raw, and later the same raw+path is used with a different ls (unlikely), we'd return the old proxy bound to old ls. Since `ls` belongs to a logic and raw+path is unique to that logic's state subtree, it's fine in practice. But after `resetContext`, a new ls may have the same raw object (unlikely). To be safe, include `ls` in the cache key? WeakMap can't key by string composite easily. Let's instead store the proxy on the `AtomicLogicState` itself: `ls.proxyCache = WeakMap<object, Map<string, any>>`. Then resetContext clears. Good.\n\nFor derived selector proxies, we don't need deep proxy - just a shallow proxy that records `selector:name` on any property access. We can also cache per selector via `meta.proxy`.\n\nLet me now write the tracker:\n\n```ts\nclass Tracker {\n  ls: AtomicLogicState\n  deps = new Map<string, AtomicDependency>()\n  constructor(ls) { this.ls = ls }\n  addLeaf(segments: DepSegment[], value: any) {\n    const id = segmentsToId(segments)\n    if (!this.deps.has(id)) this.deps.set(id, { id, type: 'leaf', segments, value: unwrapProxy(value) })\n    // if already present, maybe update value? keep first\n  }\n  addSelector(name: string, value: any) {\n    const id = `selector:${name}`\n    if (!this.deps.has(id)) this.deps.set(id, { id, type: 'selector', selectorName: name, value: unwrapProxy(value) })\n  }\n  addProps(key: string, value: any) {\n    const id = `props:${key}`\n    if (!this.deps.has(id)) this.deps.set(id, { id, type: 'props', propsKey: key, value: unwrapProxy(value) })\n  }\n}\n```\n\nFor props nested, I'll use leaf-like with segments of kind prop prefixed by a special props marker. Actually simpler: for props, use `addProps` with full path string like `user.name`? Let me make props also use leaf segments but with a flag. Let me keep `addProps` for top-level and nested as `props:user.name` with segments. I'll store `segments` and a `propsBase` flag. Let's unify: Dependency has `type: 'leaf' | 'selector' | 'props'` and `segments` (for leaf/props). For leaf, segments relative to logic root. For props, segments relative to props. id: `props:` + segmentsToId(segments). Good.\n\nNow `readDependencyValue(dep, ls, state, props)`:\n- if dep.type === 'leaf': `raw = getLogicRootRaw(ls.logic, state)`; for each segment: \n  - prop/index: `raw = raw[seg.key]`\n  - mapKey: `raw = raw.get(seg.key)`\n  - setValue: not a value read; for set membership we compare `raw.has(seg.value)`? For Set membership dependency, the \"value\" should be the boolean membership result. So when recording `set:a`, store value = `target.has(a)` (boolean). For reading back, compute `raw.has(a)`. Let's store `value` as the boolean for set. Similarly for Map `has`, value is boolean. For Map `get`, value is the value.\n  So `readDependencyValue` for setValue: return `raw.has(seg.value)`; for mapKey: return `raw.get(seg.key)`; for index/prop: `raw[seg.key]`.\n- if dep.type === 'selector': target meta = `ls.selectors[dep.selectorName]`; return target.value (raw).\n- if dep.type === 'props': read from `props` similarly (only prop/index segments).\n\nNow, comparison: use `===` (or Object.is). For custom memoize options, we could use `resultEqualityCheck` for selector deps. Let's keep `===` for leaf and props, and for selector deps use the dependency selector's `memoizeOptions.resultEqualityCheck` if provided (to handle custom memoization). Actually the existing custom memoization test expects `reversedValuesIfLengthChanges` to NOT recompute when length unchanged even though the array reference changed. In atomic mode, `reversedValuesIfLengthChanges` depends on `selector:values` (values is a reducer selector). When values array reference changes (new array same length), the reducer selector `values` re-evaluates (its dep `values` changed) and its value (raw array) changes reference. Then `reversedValuesIfLengthChanges`'s dep `selector:values` value changed. If we compare with `===`, it would recompute. But with `resultEqualityCheck` comparing lengths, it should NOT recompute. So we must use the dependency selector's memoizeOptions.resultEqualityCheck when comparing selector deps. Let me implement:\n```ts\nfunction valuesEqual(dep, current, meta) {\n  if (dep.type === 'selector') {\n    const target = ls.selectors[dep.selectorName]\n    const eq = target?.memoizeOptions?.resultEqualityCheck\n    if (eq) return eq(dep.value, current)\n  }\n  return Object.is(dep.value, current)\n}\n```\nBut the equality function signature in reselect is `(a, b)` where a is previous result. We call `eq(dep.value, current)`. Good.\n\nHowever, the propagation marking uses the *selector's own* result equality for dependents. In `evaluateOne`, after computing new value, if `oldValue !== newValue` (or `!resultEqualityCheck(old, new)`), mark dependents dirty. Use meta.memoizeOptions.resultEqualityCheck.\n\nNow, there's a subtle issue with selector deps: when we compare `selector:values`, we compare target's raw value. But if target has `resultEqualityCheck`, we use it. Good.\n\nBut wait, `reversedValuesIfLengthChanges`'s dependency on `values` is recorded via the derived proxy handler. `s.values` is a reducer selector (rootDerived), so its proxy is a state proxy, and reading `reversedValues`'s compute does `[...values]` (spread) which records all indices of `values` (leaf deps `values.0`, `values.1`, ...) rather than `selector:values`. Hmm. In the existing test:\n```js\nselectors({\n  reversedValues: [(s) => [s.values], (values) => [...values].reverse()],\n  reversedValuesIfLengthChanges: [\n    (s) => [s.values],\n    (values) => [...values].reverse(),\n    { resultEqualityCheck: (a, b) => a.length === b.length },\n  ],\n})\n```\n`values` is a reducer selector (rootDerived). The compute `[...values].reverse()` reads the array via spread, which records leaf deps `values.0`, `values.1`, etc. When `values` array reference changes (new array same length), the leaf dep `values.0` value might be the same element (if elements unchanged). So `reversedValuesIfLengthChanges` would NOT be marked dirty (since leaf deps unchanged) even without resultEqualityCheck! That's actually the desired behavior and even finer than the original. But if elements change, leaf deps change. For the test `setValue(1, 'SECOND')`, `values` becomes `['first', 'SECOND']`, so `values.1` changes -> dirty -> recompute -> new result `['SECOND','first']`; resultEqualityCheck compares length (2===2) => same result, so dependents (none) not marked. Good.\n\nSo for rootDerived deps, we use leaf tracking and don't need resultEqualityCheck. But `resultEqualityCheck` is still useful for comparing the selector's *result* when propagating to dependents (e.g., if `reversedValues` had a dependent). Let me support it anyway.\n\nNow, there's a problem with spread on arrays: our `Symbol.iterator` wrapper must record all indices and yield tracked values. Let me implement that.\n\nLet me now write the proxy handlers in detail.\n\n#### objectHandler\n\n```ts\nconst objectHandler = (ls: AtomicLogicState, base: DepSegment[]) => ({\n  get(target, prop, receiver) {\n    if (prop === RAW) return target\n    if (typeof prop === 'symbol') {\n      if (prop === Symbol.iterator && !Array.isArray(target) && !(target instanceof Map) && !(target instanceof Set)) {\n        // object iteration via for...of? plain objects aren't iterable. Return as is.\n      }\n      return Reflect.get(target, prop, target)\n    }\n    const value = Reflect.get(target, prop, target)\n    const segment = { kind: 'prop', key: prop }\n    recordLeaf(ls, [...base, segment], value)\n    return wrapReadValue(ls, value, [...base, segment])\n  },\n  has(target, prop) {\n    if (typeof prop === 'symbol') return Reflect.has(target, prop)\n    recordLeaf(ls, [...base, { kind: 'prop', key: prop }], Reflect.get(target, prop))\n    return Reflect.has(target, prop)\n  },\n  ownKeys(target) {\n    const keys = Reflect.ownKeys(target)\n    for (const key of keys) {\n      if (typeof key === 'string') {\n        recordLeaf(ls, [...base, { kind: 'prop', key }], Reflect.get(target, key))\n      }\n    }\n    return keys\n  },\n  getOwnPropertyDescriptor(target, prop) {\n    if (typeof prop === 'string') {\n      recordLeaf(ls, [...base, { kind: 'prop', key: prop }], Reflect.get(target, prop))\n    }\n    return Reflect.getOwnPropertyDescriptor(target, prop)\n  },\n})\n```\n\nWait, `recordLeaf` should only record if there's an active tracker for this ls. Let me define:\n```ts\nfunction recordLeaf(ls, segments, value) {\n  const t = activeTracker\n  if (t && t.ls === ls) {\n    t.addLeaf(segments, value)\n  }\n}\n```\n\nFor `ownKeys`, recording every key on `Object.keys(user)` is correct (selector accesses all keys). But note: `Object.keys` on a proxy calls `ownKeys` then `getOwnPropertyDescriptor` for each, so we'd record twice - fine.\n\n#### arrayHandler\n\nExtends objectHandler but:\n- For numeric index props, segment kind = 'index'.\n- For `length`, segment kind = 'prop' (or 'index'? let's use prop `length`).\n- Methods: return wrapped functions.\n- Symbol.iterator: wrapped iterator recording all indices.\n\nLet me write:\n```ts\nconst arrayHandler = (ls, base) => {\n  const obj = objectHandler(ls, base)\n  return {\n    ...obj,\n    get(target, prop, receiver) {\n      if (prop === RAW) return target\n      if (typeof prop === 'symbol') {\n        if (prop === Symbol.iterator) {\n          return function arrayIterator() {\n            return wrapArrayIterator(ls, base, target)\n          }\n        }\n        return Reflect.get(target, prop, target)\n      }\n      const str = String(prop)\n      const idx = isArrayIndex(str)\n      if (idx !== null) {\n        const segment = { kind: 'index', key: idx }\n        const value = target[idx]\n        recordLeaf(ls, [...base, segment], value)\n        return wrapReadValue(ls, value, [...base, segment])\n      }\n      // methods\n      if (typeof target[prop] === 'function') {\n        return wrapArrayMethod(ls, base, target, prop)\n      }\n      // length or other props\n      const value = target[prop]\n      const segment = { kind: 'prop', key: prop }\n      recordLeaf(ls, [...base, segment], value)\n      return wrapReadValue(ls, value, [...base, segment])\n    },\n  }\n}\n```\n\n`isArrayIndex(str)`:\n```ts\nfunction isArrayIndex(str: string): number | null {\n  if (str === '') return null\n  const n = Number(str)\n  return Number.isInteger(n) && n >= 0 && String(n) === str ? n : null\n}\n```\n\n`wrapArrayMethod(ls, base, target, prop)`:\n```ts\nfunction wrapArrayMethod(ls, base, target, prop) {\n  switch (prop) {\n    case 'includes':\n      return (searchElement: any, fromIndex?: number) => {\n        const start = fromIndex === undefined ? 0 : Math.max(fromIndex >= 0 ? fromIndex : target.length + fromIndex, 0)\n        for (let i = start; i < target.length; i++) {\n          recordLeaf(ls, [...base, { kind: 'index', key: i }], target[i])\n          if (Object.is(target[i], searchElement)) return true\n        }\n        return false\n      }\n    case 'indexOf':\n      return (searchElement: any, fromIndex?: number) => {\n        const start = fromIndex === undefined ? 0 : Math.max(fromIndex >= 0 ? fromIndex : target.length + fromIndex, 0)\n        for (let i = start; i < target.length; i++) {\n          recordLeaf(ls, [...base, { kind: 'index', key: i }], target[i])\n          if (target[i] === searchElement) return i\n        }\n        return -1\n      }\n    case 'lastIndexOf':\n      return (searchElement: any, fromIndex?: number) => {\n        const start = fromIndex === undefined ? target.length - 1 : Math.min(fromIndex >= 0 ? fromIndex : target.length + fromIndex, target.length - 1)\n        for (let i = start; i >= 0; i--) {\n          recordLeaf(ls, [...base, { kind: 'index', key: i }], target[i])\n          if (target[i] === searchElement) return i\n        }\n        return -1\n      }\n    case 'map':\n    case 'filter':\n      return (cb: any, thisArg?: any) => {\n        const result = []\n        for (let i = 0; i < target.length; i++) {\n          recordLeaf(ls, [...base, { kind: 'index', key: i }], target[i])\n          const item = wrapReadValue(ls, target[i], [...base, { kind: 'index', key: i }])\n          const mapped = cb.call(thisArg, item, i, proxyArray)\n          if (prop === 'map' || (prop === 'filter' && mapped)) result.push(unwrapProxy(mapped))\n        }\n        return prop === 'map' ? result : result // for filter, result contains raw items\n      }\n    // ... more\n    default:\n      // generic: for methods that read all, record all and call raw\n      return (...args: any[]) => {\n        for (let i = 0; i < target.length; i++) recordLeaf(ls, [...base, {kind:'index', key:i}], target[i])\n        return (target[prop] as any)(...args)\n      }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nHmm, for `map`, returning a plain array of raw results loses tracking, but map already recorded all source indices, so if any source changes, selector re-evaluates. That's conservative and correct.\n\nFor `filter`, `cb` receives tracked items; if cb accesses `item.name`, it records `list.0.name` etc. Good. Return raw filtered items.\n\nFor `some`, `every`, `find`, `findIndex`, `reduce`, `forEach`:\n```ts\ncase 'forEach':\n  return (cb, thisArg) => { for (let i=0;i<target.length;i++){ recordLeaf(...); cb.call(thisArg, wrapReadValue(...), i, proxyArray) } }\ncase 'some':\n  return (cb, thisArg) => { for (...) { recordLeaf; if (cb.call(thisArg, wrap..., i, proxyArray)) return true } return false }\ncase 'every':\n  return (cb, thisArg) => { for (...) { recordLeaf; if (!cb.call(...)) return false } return true }\ncase 'find':\n  return (cb, thisArg) => { for (...) { recordLeaf; if (cb(...)) return wrapReadValue(...) } return undefined }\ncase 'findIndex':\n  return (cb, thisArg) => { for (...) { recordLeaf; if (cb(...)) return i } return -1 }\ncase 'reduce':\n  return (cb, initial) => { let acc = arguments...; record all; acc = cb(acc, wrapReadValue(item), i, proxyArray); return acc }\ncase 'reduceRight':\n  similar reverse\n```\n\nFor `slice`, `concat`, `flat`, `flatMap`, `join`, `toString`, `toLocaleString`, `keys`, `values`, `entries`, `at`, and mutation methods, I'll implement a generic fallback that records all indices and calls the raw method. But for `at`, recording all indice