fine (idempotent-ish; evaluations count would increase though!). \n\nTo avoid double-counting evaluations, let me restructure: `recompute` should be reentrant-safe. The nested `recompute` inside `getInputValue` will evaluate the input if dirty. Then when outer `recompute` reaches that input, it's no longer dirty, so skipped. Good. But nested recompute could evaluate a selector that outer already processed (via a different path) - evaluations count increments twice in one action. Is that acceptable? \"Atomic Updates: Multiple dependency changes within a single action must trigger exactly one re-evaluation of a dependent selector.\" Double evaluation due to nested recompute could violate this. Let me make `recompute` share a \"processing\" set across nested calls.\n\nI'll store a \"recompute session\" on the logic state:\n```ts\ninterface AtomicLogicState {\n  ...\n  recomputeSession: { processed: Set<string>, state: any, props: any } | null\n}\n```\n\n`recompute(ls, state, props)`:\n```ts\nfunction recompute(ls, state, props) {\n  if (ls.recomputeSession && ls.recomputeSession.state === state) {\n    // nested recompute for same state; process remaining\n    return recomputeInSession(ls, ls.recomputeSession)\n  }\n  // mark first-eval as dirty\n  for (const meta of Object.values(ls.metas)) if (!meta.hasEvaluated) meta.dirty = true\n  const session = { processed: new Set<string>(), state, props }\n  ls.recomputeSession = session\n  try {\n    recomputeInSession(ls, session)\n  } finally {\n    ls.recomputeSession = null\n  }\n  // final check\n  for (const meta of Object.values(ls.metas)) {\n    if (meta.dirty) throw new Error('[KEA] Circular dependency detected')\n  }\n}\n```\n\n`recomputeInSession`:\n```ts\nfunction recomputeInSession(ls, session) {\n  let progressed = true\n  while (progressed) {\n    progressed = false\n    const order = buildTopologicalOrder(ls)\n    for (const name of order) {\n      const meta = ls.metas[name]\n      if (meta.dirty && !session.