\nAnd in `evaluateOne`, for local atomic inputs, call `getLocalAtomicValue` instead of `fn(state, props)` (which calls `evaluateAtomicSelector`). For placeholders, `fn._keaAtomicMeta` gives name, so we can call `getLocalAtomicValue(ls, name, state, props)`.\n\nBut the inputFn in `meta.inputFns` is the placeholder function, which has metadata. Let me use metadata to call `getLocalAtomicValue` for local selectors, avoiding the placeholder forwarding (which would call `evaluateAtomicSelector`). Actually `getInputValue` already has the metadata; let me modify it to call `getLocalAtomicValue` for local atomic selectors.\n\nLet me refactor `getInputValue`:\n```ts\nfunction getInputValue(ls, fn, state, props) {\n  const m = fn._keaAtomicMeta\n  if (m && m.props) {\n    const value = fn(state, props) // props selector doesn't evaluate; returns proxy\n    return { value, baseDep: ... }\n  }\n  if (m && m.logic === ls.logic) {\n    const value = getLocalAtomicValue(ls, m.name, state, props)\n    ...\n  }\n  // foreign/plain\n  const value = unwrapProxy(fn(state, props))\n  ...\n}\n```\n\n`getLocalAtomicValue` uses `evaluateDirty` (recursive) which avoids the nested recompute issue. Let me restructure `recompute` to use a shared session and `evaluateDirty`:\n\n```ts\nfunction createSession(ls, state, props) {\n  for (const meta of Object.values(ls.metas)) if (!meta.hasEvaluated) meta.dirty = true\n  const session = { processed: new Set<string>(), state, props }\n  ls.recomputeSession = session\n  return session\n}\n\nfunction recompute(ls, state, props) {\n  const isNested = ls.recomputeSession !== null && ls.recomputeSession.state === state\n  const session = isNested ? ls.recomputeSession! : createSession(ls, state, props)\n  const order = buildTopologicalOrder(ls)\n  for (const name of order) {\n    evaluateDirty(ls, name, state, props, session)\n  }\n  if (!isNested) {\n    ls.recomputeSession = null\n    for (const meta of Object.values(ls.metas)) {\n      if (meta.dirty)